MEDIUM-01: Token Request Endpoint Information Disclosure
========================================
Severity: MEDIUM
CVSS 4.0: 4.3 (AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:L/VI:N/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N)
CVSS 3.1: 5.3 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N)

Description:
The token request endpoint at https://main.realtime.ably.net/keys/{keyId}/requestToken returns detailed error messages that disclose:
- Whether an application/key ID exists
- Internal server IDs
- Cluster information

This enables application/key ID enumeration.

Evidence:
$ curl -X POST "https://main.realtime.ably.net/keys/nonexistent/requestToken" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"ttl": 3600000, "capability": "{\"*\":[\"*\"]}"}'

Response:
{
    "error": {
        "message": "No application found with id te. (See https://help.ably.io/error/40400 for help.)",
        "code": 40400,
        "statusCode": 404,
        "nonfatal": false,
        "href": "https://help.ably.io/error/40400",
        "serverId": "frontend.2bbe.2.eu-central-1-A.i-0a32e96cd893cef86.e91MxnBpgC9A5F"
    }
}

The error message "No application found with id te" confirms the key ID prefix "te" is invalid, allowing enumeration of valid key prefixes.

Impact:
- Application/key ID enumeration
- Internal infrastructure disclosure (server IDs, clusters)
- Targeted attacks against known applications

Remediation:
- Return generic error messages for invalid key IDs
- Do not disclose internal server IDs in error responses
- Implement rate limiting on token request endpoint

========================================
LOW-01: Missing Rate Limiting on Public /time Endpoint
========================================
Severity: LOW
CVSS 4.0: 2.6 (AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:L/VI:N/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N)
CVSS 3.1: 2.6 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N)

Description:
The public /time endpoint at https://main.realtime.ably.net/time and https://rest.ably.io/time has no apparent rate limiting, allowing unlimited requests.

Evidence:
$ for i in {1..100}; do curl -s https://main.realtime.ably.net/time; done
All 100 requests succeeded with 200 OK.

Impact:
- Potential DoS via resource exhaustion
- Timestamp oracle for timing attacks
- Abuse as free time service

Remediation:
- Implement rate limiting (e.g., 60 req/min per IP)
- Add CAPTCHA or proof-of-work for excessive requests
- Monitor for anomalous patterns

